-- Pong — the fixture the prototype exists to run.
--
-- Nothing here names a console. Two unit systems do the work:
--
--   * absolute — `1 cell` is one 8x8 hardware cell. Use it where a thing is
--     what it is everywhere: a one-tile ball, a one-tile-tall paddle, a score
--     in the corner.
--   * relative — `15vw` is 15% of the playfield's width, `40vmin` is 40% of its
--     shorter side. Use it for anything that has to stay *balanced*: a paddle
--     must cover the same fraction of the wall, and a rally must take the same
--     number of seconds, whether the court is 20 cells wide or 40.
--
-- Sizes quantise to whole cells; speeds and positions do not.

start title

-- The opponent's aim wanders, and a wandering aim needs a generator. Fixing it
-- here rather than leaving it at the default is the same reason the runner does
-- it: the seed is part of the game, so a replay is a replay.
seed 20260726

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- title -----

scene title

-- The picture is demade by the image engine, at the screen's own size, into a
-- tilemap the background layer draws for nothing. It is the same fitter a
-- photograph goes through — which is the point: a title screen is the first
-- full-bleed thing this tool was ever pointed at.
backdrop pong.title.svg

create text prompt in title (x centerx - 7, y screenheight - 2, text "press a to play")

when a pressed in title then scene as play

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------- play -----

scene play

-- The playfield is demade too, at the screen's own size, by the same fitter the
-- title screen went through. It is scenery and nothing else: the game reads
-- nothing from it and collides with none of it — what it costs is tiles.
backdrop pong.play.svg

-- And the music is demade too, by the same arranger `demake arrange` uses: a
-- four-part MIDI fitted to the console's own chip, its tempo held. It belongs
-- to the scene, so a rally has a theme and the title screen does not.
music rally.mid in play

-- Effects fire on the triggers rules already have. The bounce is the game's
-- one constant sound, so it is the one worth demaking well; the point is
-- louder and outranks it, which is what decides who gets the channel when a
-- rally ends on a wall.
sound bounce.wav on ball hits paddle
sound point.wav on ball hits screentop, screenbottom


-- The ball is one cell on every console — it is the smallest thing that can be
-- drawn — but it crosses the court in the same 1.8 seconds everywhere.
create object ball (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 55vmin, sprite ball.svg)

-- The paddle covers 15% of the wall and can traverse the court in 1.7 seconds,
-- on a Game Boy and on a Mega Drive alike.
create object paddle (width 15vw, height 1 cell, speed 60vw, sprite paddle.svg)

create ball ball1 in play (x centerx, y centery, direction southwest)

create paddle paddle1 in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 1)

-- The opponent is slower than the player, and that is half of its handicap:
-- with the ball at 55vmin it can be beaten to a corner. Everything else about
-- it is the same object.
create paddle paddle2 in play (x centerx, y 0, speed 32vw)

-- The other half of the handicap. `aim` is how far off the ball the opponent
-- means to meet it, redrawn on every bounce. It is a number with `visible 0`,
-- which is how a game holds a value nobody is meant to read: not drawn, not
-- collided with, not moved.
create number aim in play (value 0, visible 0)

create text you in play (x 1, y 1, text "you:")
create number score1 in play (value 0, x 6, y 1)
create text cpu in play (x screenwidth - 7, y 1, text "cpu:")
create number score2 in play (value 0, x screenwidth - 2, y 1)

-- The player drives the bottom paddle. `on hold` restores the previous value
-- when the button comes up, so releasing left leaves the paddle stopped.
control paddle1 left (xdirection -1) on hold
control paddle1 right (xdirection 1) on hold

-- The opponent is one level-triggered rule, not a special "ai" feature.
--
-- It is *proportional*, not on/off: `xdirection` is how far away the ball is,
-- measured in ticks of the paddle's own travel, clamped to full speed. Far away
-- it runs flat out; as it arrives the number shrinks and it eases in and stops
-- exactly on target. An on/off version overshoots by a tick every tick and
-- either buzzes or, with a dead zone wide enough to stop that, moves in visible
-- blocks. This does neither.
--
-- `5vw` is the gain: with the paddle at 32vw per second and 60 ticks per second
-- it covers 0.53vw per tick, so the ball is at full chase about nine ticks out
-- and eases through the last stretch. Both numbers are relative, so the feel
-- ports unchanged.
--
-- Five percent is also one whole cell on this court, and that is not an
-- accident: a divisor that is a whole number of cells takes the runtime's byte
-- divider instead of its general one, and this rule runs every tick of every
-- rally. It is the sort of thing you only find by profiling the ROM, which is
-- why doc 14 says to.
--
-- What it chases is `ball1.centerx + aim.value`, not the ball itself. A machine
-- that tracks the centre perfectly returns every ball down the same line,
-- because the bounce angle below is measured from where on the paddle the ball
-- landed — so a perfect opponent is not only unbeatable, it is dull. The offset
-- gives it a *preferred contact point* that changes each rally, which is one
-- number and the difference between a wall and a player.
when always in play then paddle2.xdirection as clamp((ball1.centerx + aim.value - paddle2.centerx) / 5vw, -1, 1)

-- Walls.
when ball hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as flip
when paddle hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0

-- The bounce. `ball` and `paddle` bind to whichever two objects collided, and
-- the horizontal component comes out of where on the paddle the ball landed.
--
-- Every contact also draws the opponent a new aim for the leg after next. Nine
-- percent of the court either way is under half a paddle, so it still gets
-- there — it just gets there off-centre, and sends the ball back at an angle
-- instead of straight down the middle. Drawing it here rather than in a rule of
-- its own is deliberate: a draw is only reproducible if *when* it happens is
-- part of the game, and "on every bounce" is the simplest such moment there is.
when ball hits paddle then (ydirection, xdirection, aim.value) as (flip, (ball.centerx - paddle.centerx) / paddle.width, random(-9, 9) * 1vw)

-- Scoring. A miss resets the ball to the middle, serving toward whoever
-- conceded, and every value in one rule is computed before any of them lands —
-- including the opponent's aim for the new rally.
when ball hits screentop then (score1.value, ball1.x, ball1.y, ball1.ydirection, aim.value) as (score1.value + 1, centerx, centery, 1, random(-9, 9) * 1vw)
when ball hits screenbottom then (score2.value, ball1.x, ball1.y, ball1.ydirection, aim.value) as (score2.value + 1, centerx, centery, -1, random(-9, 9) * 1vw)

when score1.value reaches 10 in play then scene as gameover
when score2.value reaches 10 in play then scene as gameover

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- game over ----

scene gameover

create text result in gameover (x centerx - 4, y centery, text "game over")

when a pressed in gameover then scene as play
